Step 4 — Focus Line

Max 45 characters.

Used in:
	•	invites
	•	front door

Start with:
	•	Discussing …
	•	Inside …
	•	Where …

(or a strong editorial verb)

Focus Line — Functional Role

The focus line is not a tagline.

It must instantly answer:

“What kind of conversation happens here?”

It is used in:
	•	invite previews
	•	link shares
	•	the server front door
	•	mobile discovery

It must:
	•	be understandable in under 2 seconds
	•	attract the right people
	•	repel the wrong people
	•	describe an ongoing conversation (not a topic)

Avoid:
	•	brand slogans
	•	mission statements
	•	abstract language

Prefer:
	•	editorial language
	•	active domains of discussion
	•	time-durable phrasing

Journalist mode focus-line bias

When generating focus lines for journalists / analysts:

Prioritize:
	•	“Inside …”
	•	“The … Desk”
	•	“The … Briefing”
	•	“The … Watch”
	•	“Where … is tracked”

Because these match:
	•	newsroom language
	•	ongoing coverage
	•	real-time signal behavior

Prefer place-based editorial language over analytical descriptions.
Generate 5 options.

Focus Line Iteration Bias

If the creator does not immediately approve a focus line:

Assume this is identity-critical.

Automatically generate a second, more refined set
based on the closest match.

Do not force a choice on the first pass.

